export const XDBML_REFERENCE = "# xDBML -- reference for AI assistants\n\n> xDBML is a text markup language to describe the semantics of structured and semi-structured data across relational, document, columnar, graph, view, and exchange formats. xDBML extends DBML into a true metadata and semantic data modeling language with richer support for validation, semantics, cardinality, annotations, and AI-friendly metadata, while deliberately staying readable and Git-friendly. When a user asks you to design, draft, edit, or render a schema for use with an xDBML tool, write xDBML -- not plain DBML -- and reach for the xDBML constructs below where they fit. Plain DBML still parses (it is a subset), but it misses the structures and metadata that make xDBML worth using.\n\nFollow this reference exactly. Do not borrow syntax from SQL DDL, other DBML dialects, Prisma, Spark or JSON Schema: where a habit from one of them and a rule here disagree, the rule here applies. Where xDBML accepts several spellings, write the one shown here. Each example below is checked against the xDBML parser. A block marked `// Wrong` shows a form assistants often write: its comment names the error a validator reports, or says that the form is accepted silently and means something other than intended. The `// Right` block after it is the form to write.\n\n## Ground rules\n\n- The first line of every document is `xdbml: 0.6`. Without it, a parser reads the file as plain DBML and rejects every xDBML construct.\n- Comments are `//` to the end of the line, or `/* ... */`. `#` and `--` are errors.\n- Keywords match any casing, and names are case-sensitive. Write construct keywords capitalized (`Entity`, `Table`, `View`, `Ref`), settings and type keywords in lowercase.\n- A name is a bare identifier, `[A-Za-z_][A-Za-z0-9_]*`, or it is double-quoted: `\"order details\"`. A field may take the name of any keyword, without quotes: `note`, `type`, `date`, `records`. `Note:` starts a note; `indexes {`, `constraints {`, `checks {`, `records {` and `definitions {` start blocks.\n- Entities, Views, Edges, Types, Enums and TablePartials share one namespace: two of them never have the same name.\n- Declare every entity, Enum and Type that the document names.\n- Use `Entity` for a generic entity. Target-native synonyms also work and carry intent: `Table` (relational), `Collection` (document/MongoDB), `Record` (Avro). Namespacing uses `Container`, `Schema`, `Database`, `Keyspace`, `Namespace`, `Dataset` or `Bucket`.\n- Prefer xDBML extensions over flat DBML where they fit: nested objects and arrays, polymorphism (`oneOf` / `union`), reusable `Type`s, precise min/max cardinality, `Edge`s, `View`s, supertype groups for generalization, and AI-readiness metadata (`synonyms`, `business_term`, `tags`, `granularity`).\n\n## Quoting\n\n| Delimiter | Holds | Example |\n|---|---|---|\n| `'...'` | every string: notes, defaults, patterns, cardinalities, colors, constraint names | `note: 'Billing address'` |\n| `'''...'''` | text on several lines, and text that holds a `'`, such as most SQL | `source_query: '''SELECT id FROM orders WHERE status = 'open' AND total > 0'''` |\n| `\"...\"` | a name, or a type, that is not a bare identifier | `\"order details\"`, `\"timestamp with time zone\"` |\n| `` `...` `` | an expression the database evaluates | `` default: `now()` `` |\n\n- A `'` inside `'...'` ends the string. Write it `\\'` (`'The customer\\'s number'`), or use `'''...'''`. English notes often hold one.\n- Double quotes never delimit a string: `Note: \"text\"` is an error.\n- A type written in several words is one double-quoted name: `\"double precision\"`, `\"timestamp with time zone\"`, `\"int unsigned\"`. A one-word synonym works as well: `timestamptz`, `float8`.\n- In a qualified name or a path, quote each name on its own: `\"sales\".\"Work Order\".\"Work Order ID\"`, `(\"Work Order ID\", \"Part ID\") [pk]`. A dot inside quotes is part of the name: `\"sales.Work Order\"` is one entity named `sales.Work Order`, not an entity of `sales`.\n- Colors are strings: `headercolor: '#3498DB'`, `color: '#999999'`.\n\n## Separators\n\nSettings and bodies separate their items differently:\n\n- Inside `[ ]`, a comma separates every two settings, also when each setting sits on its own line. A line break never replaces the comma.\n- Inside `{ }`, each item -- a field, an enum value, a constraint, an index, a group member -- takes its own line, with no comma or semicolon after it. A parser accepts and ignores them between the items of a body, but tools write newlines.\n\n```xdbml\nEntity customers {\n  customer_id int     [pk]\n  email       varchar [unique, not null]\n}\n\nEntity vehicles {\n  vehicle_id  int [pk]\n  customer_id int [not null]\n}\n\nRef customer_vehicles: vehicles.customer_id > customers.customer_id [\n  source: '0..*',\n  target: '1..1',\n  source_role: 'owned vehicle',\n  target_role: 'owner'\n]\n```\n\nOther lists keep their own rules. In `indexes { }` a comma between two entries is an error, and a composite index is written `(a, b)`. A `records { }` row puts a comma between its values, one row per line. `union [T1, T2]` and `map [K, V]` need their commas.\n\n## What goes where\n\nA construct takes its settings in brackets after its name, before its body: `materialized: true` inside a View body is a syntax error. A field with a body -- `object { }`, `oneOf { }`, `json { }` -- takes its settings after the closing brace. In a body, the `name: value` form is only for `Note:`, for `targets:` in a Project, and for `source_query:` in a View.\n\n| Construct | Brackets after the name | Body holds | Declared |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| `Project` | none | `targets:`, `Note:` | top level, at most once |\n| `Container`, `Schema`, `Database`, ... | `type`, `target`, `note`, custom | entities, Views, Edges, Enums, `Note:` | top level |\n| `Entity`, `Table`, `Collection`, `Record` | `note`, `headercolor`, `tags`, `synonyms`, `business_term`, custom | fields, `~partial`, `Note:`, `constraints { }`, `indexes { }`, `records { }`, `definitions { }` | top level or in a Container |\n| `View` | `materialized`, `refresh_schedule`, `refresh_on`, `source_database`, `storage_options`, `note`, `synonyms`, `business_term` | `source_query:` once, fields, `Note:`, and nothing else | top level or in a Container |\n| `Edge` | `source`, `target` (entities), `source_cardinality`, `target_cardinality`, `undirected`, roles and verbs, `note` | fields, `~partial`, `Note:`, `constraints { }`, `indexes { }` | top level or in a Container |\n| `Enum` | none | values, each with an optional `[note: '...']` | top level or in a Container |\n| `Type` | `note`, `tags`, `synonyms`, `business_term` | fields, `~partial` | top level |\n| `TablePartial` | `note`, `headercolor` | fields, `Note:`, `constraints { }`, `indexes { }` | top level |\n| `Ref` | relationship settings | -- | top level, never in a Container |\n| `SupertypeGroup` | `supertype` (required), `completeness`, `exclusivity`, `strategy`, `merge`, `discriminator`, `note` | subtype names, each with an optional `[strategy: ...]` | top level |\n| `TableGroup` | `color`, `note` | entity names | top level |\n| `DiagramView` | `note`, custom | `Note:`, and categories: `Tables`, `Views`, `Containers`, `TableGroups`, `SupertypeGroups`, `Notes`, each with names or `*` | top level, never in a Container |\n\nA note goes either in the brackets as `note: '...'` or in the body as `Note: '...'`.\n\n## Fields\n\nA field line is `name  type  [settings]`.\n\nTypes. In a physical model, use the target's own type names; any type name passes through as written: `varchar(255)`, `decimal(14,2)`, `smallint`, `serial`, `bytea`, `timestamptz` for PostgreSQL, `number(10)` and `varchar2(255)` for Oracle, `objectId`, `string`, `int32`, `int64`, `Decimal128`, `Date` for MongoDB. A logical model uses generic names: `int`, `bigint`, `varchar`, `decimal(p,s)`, `boolean`, `date`, `timestamp`. A field typed by an Enum or a Type names it (`status service_status`, `address Address`); an Enum declared in a Container is named with its qualifier (`status core.job_status`). A type name close to a declared Type or Enum (`Adress` next to `Type Address`) draws a `possible-type-typo` warning. A Type never takes the name of a built-in type, in any letter case: a field typed `money` or `Money` gets the built-in `money`, so `Type Money` is an error; name it `MonetaryAmount`.\n\nCollection and structured types use xDBML's own syntax, never the Spark, SQL or TypeScript forms:\n\n| Write | Never |\n|---|---|\n| `array [string]` | `array<string>` |\n| `object { street string }` | `struct<street:string>` |\n| `map [string, int]` | `map<string,int>` |\n| `set [varchar]` | `set<varchar>` |\n| a declared `Enum` | `enum('a', 'b')` as a type |\n\n`text[]` is PostgreSQL's own name for an array type. It passes through as a type name, and the model sees no element type in it: an array in the model is `array [T]`.\n\nSettings. These are all the field settings:\n\n- flags: `pk`, `unique`, `not null`, `null`, `increment`\n- values: `default`, `note`, `ref`, `check`\n- validation: `pattern`, `format`, `minLength`, `maxLength`, `minimum`, `maximum`, `exclusiveMinimum`, `exclusiveMaximum`, `multipleOf`, `minItems`, `maxItems`, `uniqueItems`, `minProperties`, `maxProperties`\n- AI-readiness: `tags`, `synonyms`, `business_term`, `granularity`\n- beside an inline `ref:` only: `foreign_master`\n- custom properties: any name that starts with `x_`\n\nA word from another language is not a setting. A parser keeps it as an unknown property and nothing reads it. Write `increment`, not `auto_increment`, `autoincrement` or `identity`; `note`, not `comment` or `description`; `pk`, not `primary_key`. Leave out `nullable`: a field accepts null unless it is marked `not null`. An index is an entry in `indexes { }`, not an `[index]` flag, and a relationship is a `ref:` or a `Ref`, never `fk` or `references`.\n\nValues:\n\n- `default:` takes a string in single quotes (`'US'`), a number, `true`, `false`, `null`, or a backtick expression for anything the database computes: `` `now()` ``, `` `CURRENT_TIMESTAMP` ``, `` `gen_random_uuid()` ``. `default: 'now()'` is the text `now()`, not a call.\n- `tags:` and `synonyms:` are lists, also with a single item: `tags: ['kpi']`.\n- `granularity:` takes one bare word: `year`, `quarter`, `month`, `week`, `day`, `hour`, `minute`, `second`, `millisecond`, `microsecond`, `nanosecond`.\n- `business_term:` is a string: `'Gross Order Value'`, or a glossary URL.\n- `check:` is an expression in single quotes, or in backticks when it holds a quote: `` check: `status IN ('open', 'closed')` ``.\n- A primary key field is never null, and still carries `not null`. `null` on it is an error.\n\n## Keys, checks and indexes\n\nPut a key on one field without a name inline (`[pk]`, `[unique]`). Put every named key, every key on several fields, and every check that involves several fields in the entity's `constraints` block, one per line, fields listed in key order. A check on one field goes on the field as `check:`. Declare each key once: a key written inline is not repeated in `constraints`. Never mark several fields `[pk]` for a composite key; several `[unique]` fields are separate keys, not one. Checks go in single quotes, or backticks when the expression holds a quote.\n\n```xdbml\nTable pitstops {\n  raceid   int [not null]\n  driverid int [not null]\n  stop     int [not null, check: 'stop >= 1']\n  lap      int [not null]\n  constraints {\n    (raceid, driverid, stop) [pk, name: 'pk_pitstops']\n    (raceid, driverid, lap)  [unique, name: 'uk_pitstops_lap']\n    'lap >= stop'            [name: 'chk_pitstops_lap']\n  }\n}\n```\n\nA `constraints` block appears at most once, in the body of an Entity, a TablePartial or an Edge. A View has none. A key names fields declared in the same body. A key may name a nested field (`profile.email`) but never cross an array. In a document store, keys follow the same syntax.\n\n`indexes { }` lists physical access paths, one entry per line: a field, a composite `(a, b)`, or an expression `` (`lower(email)`) ``, each with optional settings `name`, `type` (`btree`, `hash`, ...), `unique` and `note`. A key belongs in `constraints`, not in `indexes`.\n\n## Relationships\n\nA relationship between attributes names the foreign key of the child and the key of the parent it references:\n\n`Ref <name>: <child>.<foreign key> > <parent>.<key> [<settings>]`\n\nor inline on the foreign key field: `customer_id int [ref: > customers.customer_id, not null]`.\n\nOperators: `>` many-to-one, `<` one-to-many, `-` one-to-one, `<>` many-to-many. The referenced side depends on the operator: the right side of `>`, the left side of `<`, and either side of `-`. `a > b` and `b < a` declare the same relationship.\n\nThe referenced side names the parent's primary key or one of its unique keys: all the fields of that key, and no other field. When the field on the referenced side is the child's own foreign key, the operator points the wrong way. Declare the referenced field `unique` when it is not the key.\n\nCardinality: `source` describes the left endpoint and `target` the right one, each as a quoted `'min..max'` string: `'0..1'`, `'1..1'`, `'0..*'`, `'1..*'`, or `'N..M'`. With `>`, the source maximum is `*` and the target maximum is `1`; with `<`, the reverse. Never `'many'`, `'*'` or `'1'`. Without these settings, the cardinality follows from the operator and from whether the foreign key is `not null`.\n\nAll relationship settings go in the brackets of the `Ref`. An inline `ref:` takes none but `foreign_master`: other settings beside it belong to the field, not to the relationship.\n\n| Setting | Values |\n|---|---|\n| `source`, `target` | `'min..max'` |\n| `delete`, `update` | `cascade`, `restrict`, `set null`, `set default`, `no action` |\n| `source_role`, `target_role`, `source_verb`, `target_verb` | text in single quotes |\n| `constraint_type` | `identifying`, `non_identifying` |\n| `foreign_master`, `inactive` | flags |\n| `undirected` | `true`, on a relationship between entities |\n| `color` | `'#rrggbb'` |\n| `note` | text in single quotes |\n\n`Ref fk_orders_customer: ...` names the foreign key constraint. `Ref` declarations sit at the top level, never inside a Container; an endpoint in a Container is qualified with it: `Ref: sales.orders.customer_id > core.customers.id`. The endpoints of a `Ref` are entities (`Entity`, `Table`, `Collection`, `Record`) and their fields, never a View or an Edge.\n\nA composite foreign key lists its fields in parentheses on both sides, and the parent declares that key in `constraints`:\n\n```xdbml\nTable merchants {\n  merchant_id  int     [not null]\n  country_code char(2) [not null]\n  constraints {\n    (merchant_id, country_code) [pk]\n  }\n}\n\nTable merchant_periods {\n  period_id    int     [pk]\n  merchant_id  int     [not null]\n  country_code char(2) [not null]\n}\n\nRef: merchant_periods.(merchant_id, country_code) > merchants.(merchant_id, country_code)\n```\n\nA path into a nested field continues with dots: `orders.shipping.country > countries.iso_code`. A path that crosses an array marks the crossing with `.[*]`: `orders.line_items.[*].sku > products.sku`.\n\nA relationship whose referenced fields are not a key is the error `ref-target-not-key`. The rule does not apply when the referenced entity declares no key, as in a conceptual or early logical model; to `<>` and `foreign_master` relationships; to relationships between entities; or when either entity sits in a Container whose target is not relational (document, graph, key-value, serialization format).\n\nA relationship can record denormalization rather than a key reference. Add `foreign_master` when the child attribute holds a copy of the parent's value, as document models often do; nothing is generated from it, it takes one attribute on each side, and a child attribute has at most one master:\n\n```xdbml\nCollection customers {\n  customer_id objectId [pk]\n  name        string\n  address object {\n    city string\n  }\n}\n\nCollection orders {\n  order_id     objectId [pk]\n  customer_id  objectId [ref: > customers.customer_id]\n  customerName string   [ref: > customers.name, foreign_master]\n  shipTo object {\n    city string\n  }\n}\n\nRef: orders.shipTo.city > customers.address.city [foreign_master]\n```\n\nRelationships carry documentation for conceptual and logical modeling. A role names what the entity at one end plays. A verb reads the relationship in one direction: `source_verb` from the left entity toward the right one, `target_verb` from the right entity toward the left one, so this relationship reads \"an order is bought by a customer\" and \"a customer places orders\". `constraint_type` records whether the child's foreign key is part of its primary key. None of it reaches a generator:\n\n```xdbml\nEntity customers {\n  customer_id int [pk]\n}\n\nEntity orders {\n  order_id    int [pk]\n  customer_id int [not null]\n}\n\nRef places: orders.customer_id > customers.customer_id [\n  source_role: 'shopping cart',\n  source_verb: 'is bought by',\n  target_role: 'buyer',\n  target_verb: 'places',\n  constraint_type: non_identifying\n]\n```\n\nAn endpoint may also name an entity and stop there, for a relationship drawn before either entity has attributes. Such a relationship states no cardinality until one is written, takes its reading direction from the operator alone (`-` meaning linked with no direction stated, `<>` not allowed), and accepts `undirected: true` for one that reads the same way both ways:\n\n```xdbml\nEntity Customer { }\nEntity Order { }\n\nRef: Customer > Order [source_verb: 'places']\n```\n\n## Views\n\nA View's settings (`materialized`, `refresh_schedule`, `refresh_on`, `source_database`, `storage_options`) go in the brackets after the View name, before the body. The body holds the source query, once, as `source_query:`, the fields, and an optional `Note:`, and nothing else: no `constraints`, `indexes`, `checks` or `records` block. Write the source query in triple quotes, which hold the quotes of SQL literals. A `source_query` in the brackets is not the view's source query and draws a warning.\n\nA key on one field of a View is marked `[pk]`. A View declares no key on several fields, since its body holds no `constraints` block. A View is never the endpoint of a `Ref`.\n\n```xdbml\nView top_customers [materialized: true, refresh_schedule: 'daily'] {\n  source_query: '''SELECT id, SUM(total) AS ltv FROM orders WHERE status = 'paid' GROUP BY id'''\n  id  int [pk]\n  ltv decimal(15,2)\n}\n\nView center_monthly_revenue [materialized: true, refresh_schedule: 'monthly'] {\n  source_query: '''\n    SELECT center_id, date_trunc('month', issued_on) AS month, SUM(total) AS revenue\n    FROM invoices\n    GROUP BY center_id, date_trunc('month', issued_on)\n  '''\n  center_id int           [not null]\n  month     date          [not null, granularity: month]\n  revenue   decimal(14,2) [tags: ['kpi']]\n  Note: 'One row per service center and month'\n}\n```\n\n## Edges\n\nAn Edge is a relationship with its own properties -- use it, not a `Ref`, when the relationship itself carries attributes. `source:` and `target:` name the entities it connects, and its cardinality goes in `source_cardinality:` and `target_cardinality:`. In a `Ref`, `source:` and `target:` are cardinalities; in an Edge, they are entities.\n\n```xdbml\nEntity Person {\n  person_id int [pk]\n}\n\nEdge KNOWS [source: Person, target: Person, source_cardinality: '0..*', target_cardinality: '0..*'] {\n  since date [not null]\n}\n```\n\n## Supertype groups\n\nGeneralization (supertype and subtypes) is a `SupertypeGroup`: one supertype, its subtypes along one axis, and whether every supertype instance is one of them (`completeness: total | partial`) and whether it can be several (`exclusivity: disjoint | overlapping`); leave either out when it is not known. Each subtype declares only its own attributes; never repeat the supertype's, that is an error. Use one group per axis: a supertype may anchor several groups, an entity is a subtype in one group only, and a subtype may be the supertype of another group. The group name is required and names the axis:\n\n```xdbml\nEntity Party {\n  party_id int [pk]\n  name     varchar\n}\n\nEntity Person {\n  birth_date date\n}\n\nEntity Organization {\n  legal_form varchar\n}\n\nSupertypeGroup legal_nature [supertype: Party, completeness: total, exclusivity: disjoint, strategy: roll_up, merge: flat, discriminator: nature] {\n  Person\n  Organization [strategy: roll_down]\n}\n```\n\n`strategy` (`preserved_hierarchy`, `roll_up`, `roll_down`, also per subtype), `merge` (`flat`, `nested`) and `discriminator` record how a physical model is meant to be derived; they add nothing to the logical model, and `discriminator` is not allowed on an overlapping group. A path through a subtype names only the subtype's own attributes, so to point a relationship at a subtype, name the subtype as an entity: `Ref: assignments.employee_id > Employee`, not `Employee.party_id`.\n\n## Diagram views\n\nA diagram view is a named subset of the model's diagram, a subject area: it lists what to show, and declares nothing. Each category lists names, one per line, or `*` for every element of its kind: `Tables` (entities, whatever keyword declares them), `Views` (database views), `Containers`, `TableGroups`, `SupertypeGroups` and `Notes` (sticky notes). Write a name in a Container with its Container, as in `sales.orders`. Relationships and Edges are never listed: one appears in a diagram view when both of its ends are there.\n\n```xdbml\nSchema sales {\n  Table orders      { id int [pk] }\n  Table order_lines { id int [pk] }\n  Table returns     { id int [pk] }\n}\n\nSchema billing {\n  Table invoices { id int [pk] }\n}\n\nSchema crm {\n  Table customers { id int [pk] }\n  Table leads     { id int [pk] }\n}\n\nDiagramView order_flow {\n  Note: 'From the order to its invoice and payment'\n  Containers {\n    sales\n    billing\n  }\n  Tables {\n    sales.orders\n    sales.order_lines\n    crm.customers\n  }\n}\n```\n\nA listed Container brings all of its entities, unless `Tables` names some of them: then it brings only those. Here `sales` brings `orders` and `order_lines` and leaves `returns` out, `billing` brings every entity, and `crm`, not listed, contributes `customers` alone. `Views` narrows the database views of a listed Container the same way, separately. A listed TableGroup or supertype group brings all of its members, whichever Container holds them. Each category appears once, and a diagram view has a unique name.\n\n## Nesting and polymorphism\n\n```xdbml\nCollection orders {\n  _id        objectId [pk]\n  shipping object {\n    street  string [not null]\n    country string\n  } [not null]\n  line_items array [\n    line_item object {\n      sku   string     [not null]\n      qty   int32      [minimum: 1]\n      price Decimal128\n    }\n  ]\n  payment oneOf {\n    card object {\n      last4 string\n    }\n    bank object {\n      iban string\n    }\n  } [discriminator: kind]\n  score union [int, decimal, null]\n  flags map [string, bool]\n  labels array [string] [maxItems: 10]\n}\n```\n\nThe settings of a field with a body come after its closing brace. `array [T]` holds one element type; a comma list of scalars, `array [int, string]`, is shorthand for `array [union [int, string]]`, and a mix of object shapes is a `oneOf`. `json` stores an opaque JSON value; `json { ... }` describes its shape.\n\n## Internal definitions\n\nA shape reused inside one entity goes in the entity's `definitions { }` block, as JSON Schema keeps it under `$defs`. Each entry is written like a `Type` without the keyword: a name and a body of fields, or a name, a type and settings. The fields of the entity, and the other entries, name it like any type.\n\n```xdbml\nProject crm {\n  targets: 'JSON Schema'\n}\n\nEntity customers {\n  id       string  [not null]\n  billing  Address [not null]\n  contacts array [Contact]\n\n  definitions {\n    Address {\n      street  string [not null]\n      country CountryCode\n    }\n    CountryCode string [pattern: '^[A-Z]{2}$']\n    Contact {\n      kind    string [not null]\n      address Address\n    }\n  }\n}\n```\n\nAn internal definition is visible only inside its entity, so two entities may each declare an `Address` of a different shape. A shape reused across entities is a `Type` at the top level. JSON Schema, Avro, Protobuf and document targets such as MongoDB accept the block. Relational targets, Cassandra, ScyllaDB, Neo4j, Memgraph, Neptune and JanusGraph take none: there, a reused shape is a `Type`. An entity holds at most one `definitions` block, and a TablePartial, an Edge or a View holds none.\n\n## Partials\n\nA `TablePartial` declares a set of fields once, for several bodies. A `~name` line places them where it is written, in the order the TablePartial declares them: in an Entity or an Edge, among the fields of an `object { }` or a `json { }`, or in the body of a `Type`. A field the body declares itself overrides an injected field of the same name; among partials, the last one injected applies. At the top level of an Entity or an Edge, a partial also brings the keys and checks of its `constraints { }`, the entries of its `indexes { }`, and its `headercolor` and its note where the entity declares none. In a nested body it brings its fields only. The body of a TablePartial holds no `~name` line, at any depth. `~` never names a `Type`: a Type is the type of a field (`billing Address`), and the field holds its shape as a nested value.\n\n```xdbml\nTablePartial audit {\n  created_at timestamp [not null]\n  created_by varchar\n}\n\nEntity invoices {\n  invoice_id int [pk]\n  ~audit\n  total      decimal(12,2) [not null]\n}\n\nEntity shipments {\n  shipment_id int [pk]\n  delivery object {\n    ~audit\n    carrier varchar\n  }\n}\n```\n\n## Projects and containers\n\n```xdbml\nProject fleet {\n  targets: [PostgreSQL, MongoDB]\n  Note: 'Vehicles in PostgreSQL, telemetry in MongoDB'\n}\n\nSchema core [target: PostgreSQL] {\n  Enum fuel_type {\n    petrol\n    diesel\n    electric\n  }\n  Table vehicles {\n    vehicle_id int            [pk]\n    fuel       core.fuel_type [not null]\n  }\n}\n\nDatabase telemetry [target: MongoDB] {\n  Collection readings {\n    _id        objectId [pk]\n    vehicle_id int      [not null]\n    taken_at   Date     [not null, granularity: second]\n  }\n}\n\nRef: telemetry.readings.vehicle_id > core.vehicles.vehicle_id\n```\n\nWith one target in the Project, every Container inherits it. With several, every Container declares `target:`, one of the Project's targets. A Container holds entities, Views, Edges, Enums and notes; Types, Refs, groups and partials stay at the top level.\n\n## AI-readiness and custom metadata\n\n```xdbml\nEntity customers [x_owner: 'data-team@acme.com', x_retention_days: 2555] {\n  id  int     [pk]\n  mrr decimal [synonyms: ['monthly revenue'], business_term: 'MRR', tags: ['kpi'], granularity: month]\n}\n```\n\n## Common mistakes\n\nA missing comma between settings on separate lines.\n\n```xdbml\nEntity customers {\n  customer_id int [pk]\n}\n\nEntity vehicles {\n  vehicle_id  int [pk]\n  customer_id int [not null]\n}\n\n// Wrong (parse-error): a line break does not separate settings\nRef customer_vehicles: vehicles.customer_id > customers.customer_id [\n  source: '0..*'\n  target: '1..1'\n]\n\n// Right\nRef customer_vehicles: vehicles.customer_id > customers.customer_id [\n  source: '0..*',\n  target: '1..1'\n]\n```\n\nA reversed relationship. The right side names the child's foreign key instead of the parent's key.\n\n```xdbml\nTable service_orders {\n  order_id int [pk]\n}\n\nTable warranties {\n  warranty_id int [pk]\n  order_id    int [not null]\n}\n\n// Wrong (ref-target-not-key): with <, the left side is the referenced side\nRef: warranties.order_id < service_orders.order_id\n\n// Right: with >, the right side is the referenced side\nRef: warranties.order_id > service_orders.order_id\n\n// Right: the same relationship, written with <\nRef: service_orders.order_id < warranties.order_id\n```\n\nA foreign key to one field of a composite key.\n\n```xdbml\nTable order_lines {\n  order_id int [not null]\n  line_no  int [not null]\n  constraints {\n    (order_id, line_no) [pk]\n  }\n}\n\nTable shipments {\n  shipment_id int [pk]\n  order_id    int [not null]\n  line_no     int [not null]\n}\n\n// Wrong (ref-target-not-key): order_lines.order_id is one of the two fields of the key\nRef: shipments.order_id > order_lines.order_id\n\n// Right\nRef: shipments.(order_id, line_no) > order_lines.(order_id, line_no)\n```\n\nA block in a View body.\n\n```xdbml\n// Wrong (parse-error): a View takes no constraints block\nView center_monthly_revenue [materialized: true] {\n  source_query: '''SELECT center_id, month FROM monthly_totals'''\n  center_id int  [not null]\n  month     date [not null]\n  constraints {\n    (center_id, month) [pk]\n  }\n}\n\n// Right\nView center_monthly_revenue [materialized: true] {\n  source_query: '''SELECT center_id, month FROM monthly_totals'''\n  center_id int  [not null]\n  month     date [not null]\n  Note: 'One row per service center and month'\n}\n```\n\nA setting in a body.\n\n```xdbml\n// Wrong (parse-error): settings go in the brackets\nView monthly_revenue {\n  materialized: true\n  source_query: 'SELECT month, SUM(total) AS revenue FROM orders GROUP BY month'\n  month date [pk]\n}\n\n// Right\nView monthly_revenue [materialized: true] {\n  source_query: 'SELECT month, SUM(total) AS revenue FROM orders GROUP BY month'\n  month date [pk]\n}\n```\n\nA `Ref` inside a Container.\n\n```xdbml\n// Wrong (parse-error): a Ref is a top-level declaration\nSchema shop {\n  Table customers {\n    id int [pk]\n  }\n  Table orders {\n    id          int [pk]\n    customer_id int [not null]\n  }\n  Ref: orders.customer_id > customers.id\n}\n\n// Right\nSchema shop {\n  Table customers {\n    id int [pk]\n  }\n  Table orders {\n    id          int [pk]\n    customer_id int [not null]\n  }\n}\n\nRef: shop.orders.customer_id > shop.customers.id\n```\n\nRelationship settings beside an inline `ref:`.\n\n```xdbml\nTable customers {\n  id int [pk]\n}\n\n// Wrong (accepted silently): delete: cascade becomes a property of the field, and the relationship has none\nTable orders {\n  id          int [pk]\n  customer_id int [ref: > customers.id, delete: cascade]\n}\n\n// Right\nTable orders {\n  id          int [pk]\n  customer_id int [not null]\n}\n\nRef: orders.customer_id > customers.id [delete: cascade]\n```\n\nA quote inside a string, and a function call as a default.\n\n```xdbml\n// Wrong (lex-error): the apostrophe ends the string\nTable customers {\n  id int [pk, note: 'The customer's number']\n}\n\n// Wrong (parse-error): a function call is an expression, written in backticks\nTable events {\n  id         int       [pk]\n  created_at timestamp [default: now()]\n}\n\n// Right\nTable customers {\n  id int [pk, note: 'The customer\\'s number']\n}\n\nTable events {\n  id         int       [pk]\n  created_at timestamp [default: `now()`]\n}\n```\n\nTypes from other languages.\n\n```xdbml\n// Wrong (parse-error): Spark generics and unquoted multi-word types\nTable readings {\n  id       int [pk]\n  samples  array<double>\n  taken_at timestamp with time zone\n}\n\n// Wrong (accepted silently): string[] passes through as a type name, not as an array\nTable readings {\n  id     int [pk]\n  labels string[]\n}\n\n// Right\nTable readings {\n  id       int [pk]\n  samples  array [double]\n  taken_at \"timestamp with time zone\"\n  labels   array [string]\n}\n```\n\nSettings from other languages.\n\n```xdbml\n// Wrong (accepted silently): none of these is an xDBML setting\nTable users {\n  id    int     [pk, auto_increment]\n  email varchar [nullable, comment: 'Login address']\n}\n\n// Right\nTable users {\n  id    int     [pk, increment]\n  email varchar [note: 'Login address']\n}\n```\n\nCardinality in the entity settings of an Edge.\n\n```xdbml\nEntity Person {\n  person_id int [pk]\n}\n\n// Wrong (accepted silently): source and target name entities in an Edge\nEdge KNOWS [source: Person, target: Person, source: '0..*', target: '0..*'] {\n  since date\n}\n\n// Right\nEdge KNOWS [source: Person, target: Person, source_cardinality: '0..*', target_cardinality: '0..*'] {\n  since date\n}\n```\n\nA path across an array without `.[*]`.\n\n```xdbml\nCollection products {\n  sku string [pk]\n}\n\nCollection orders {\n  _id        objectId [pk]\n  line_items array [\n    line_item object {\n      sku string [not null]\n    }\n  ]\n}\n\n// Wrong (invalid-nested-path): the path crosses the array without saying so\nRef: orders.line_items.sku > products.sku\n\n// Right\nRef: orders.line_items.[*].sku > products.sku\n```\n\nA key declared twice. Keep `[pk]` or `[unique]` on a single unnamed field, and use `constraints` only for a named key or a key on several fields.\n\n```xdbml\n// Wrong (duplicate-primary-key): the primary key is declared twice\nTable customers {\n  customer_id int [pk]\n  constraints {\n    (customer_id) [pk]\n  }\n}\n\n// Right\nTable customers {\n  customer_id int [pk]\n}\n```\n\nA composite key written as several `[pk]` fields. Write one key line in `constraints`.\n\n```xdbml\n// Wrong (accepted silently): accepted for DBML compatibility, never written\nTable enrollments {\n  student_id int [pk]\n  course_id  int [pk]\n}\n\n// Right\nTable enrollments {\n  student_id int [not null]\n  course_id  int [not null]\n  constraints {\n    (student_id, course_id) [pk]\n  }\n}\n```\n\nSeparators between items. Write one field or enum value per line, with nothing after it. Quote an enum value only when it holds characters outside an identifier, and then in double quotes.\n\n```xdbml\n// Wrong (accepted silently): accepted and ignored, never written\nEnum service_status {\n  'scheduled' [note: 'Appointment booked'],\n  'on_hold' [note: 'Awaiting parts'],\n}\n\n// Right\nEnum service_status {\n  scheduled [note: 'Appointment booked']\n  on_hold   [note: 'Awaiting parts']\n  \"on hold (legacy)\"\n}\n```\n\nA comma between index entries. List one index per line, and write a composite index in parentheses.\n\n```xdbml\n// Wrong (parse-error): a comma between two index entries\nTable people {\n  id    int [pk]\n  email varchar\n  name  varchar\n  indexes {\n    email, name\n  }\n}\n\n// Right: two indexes, then one composite index\nTable people {\n  id    int [pk]\n  email varchar\n  name  varchar\n  indexes {\n    email\n    name\n    (email, name)\n  }\n}\n```\n\nNaming a Type after a built-in type. `money`, `date`, `json`, `uuid` and the other built-in types take precedence over a Type of the same name, whatever its letter case, so the fields would never reach the Type.\n\n```xdbml\n// Wrong (named-type-shadows-builtin): money is a built-in type\nType Money {\n  amount   decimal(19,4) [not null]\n  currency varchar(3)    [not null]\n}\n\n// Right\nType MonetaryAmount {\n  amount   decimal(19,4) [not null]\n  currency varchar(3)    [not null]\n}\n```\n\nListing relationships in a diagram view. A diagram view has no `Edges` or `Refs` category: a relationship or an Edge appears when both of its ends are members.\n\n```xdbml\nEntity customers {\n  id int [pk]\n}\n\nEntity orders {\n  id          int [pk]\n  customer_id int [not null, ref: > customers.id]\n}\n\n// Wrong (parse-error): Edges is not a DiagramView category\nDiagramView ordering {\n  Tables {\n    customers\n    orders\n  }\n  Edges {\n    *\n  }\n}\n\n// Right\nDiagramView ordering {\n  Tables {\n    customers\n    orders\n  }\n}\n```\n\nWriting `$defs` for internal definitions. xDBML writes the block `definitions`, and `$` is not a character of the language.\n\n```xdbml\n// Wrong (lex-error): $defs is JSON Schema syntax\nEntity customers {\n  billing Address\n  $defs {\n    Address {\n      street string\n    }\n  }\n}\n\n// Right\nEntity customers {\n  billing Address\n  definitions {\n    Address {\n      street string\n    }\n  }\n}\n```\n\nInternal definitions for a relational target. A relational database reuses a shape as a user-defined type of the schema, which a `Type` at the top level declares.\n\n```xdbml\nProject erp {\n  targets: PostgreSQL\n}\n\n// Wrong (definitions-unsupported-target): a relational target takes no internal definitions\nTable customers {\n  id      int [pk]\n  billing Address\n  definitions {\n    Address {\n      street varchar(100)\n    }\n  }\n}\n\n// Right\nType Address {\n  street varchar(100)\n}\n\nTable customers {\n  id      int [pk]\n  billing Address\n}\n```\n\nNaming an internal definition outside its entity. Another entity does not see it: the name passes through as a target-native type, with no shape, and a qualified name such as `customers.Address` does the same.\n\n```xdbml\nEntity customers {\n  billing Address\n  definitions {\n    Address {\n      street string\n    }\n  }\n}\n\n// Wrong (accepted silently): suppliers.address has no shape\nEntity suppliers {\n  address customers.Address\n}\n\n// Right\nEntity suppliers {\n  address Address\n  definitions {\n    Address {\n      street string\n    }\n  }\n}\n```\n\n## Checklist before you answer\n\n1. The first line is `xdbml: 0.6`.\n2. Every `[ ]` holding two or more settings has a comma between each two of them, across line breaks too.\n3. No comma or semicolon follows an item in `{ }`.\n4. In every relationship between attributes, the referenced side -- the right side of `>`, the left side of `<` -- names all the fields of the parent's primary key or of one of its unique keys, and no other field.\n5. Relationship settings sit in a `Ref`, not beside an inline `ref:`. Cardinalities are quoted `'min..max'` strings: `source` for the left end, `target` for the right end.\n6. `Ref`, `Type`, `SupertypeGroup`, `TableGroup`, `TablePartial` and `DiagramView` declarations sit at the top level.\n7. A View body holds `source_query:`, fields and `Note:`, and nothing else.\n8. A key sits inline only on one unnamed field, and otherwise in `constraints`; each key is declared once, and primary key fields carry `not null`.\n9. Strings use single quotes, with each `'` inside escaped or the string in `'''...'''`; database expressions use backticks; multi-word types use double quotes.\n10. Every setting comes from this reference, or starts with `x_`. `tags` and `synonyms` are lists.\n11. Edge cardinality uses `source_cardinality` and `target_cardinality`.\n12. Every entity, field, Enum and Type the document names is declared, qualified with its Container where it sits in one.\n13. Internal definitions sit in one `definitions { }` block of the entity that uses them, never for a relational, Cassandra, ScyllaDB or graph target; a shape used by several entities is a top-level `Type`.\n\nTo check a schema, run it through an xDBML validator when one is available, or paste it into the playground at https://xdbml.org/playground/: its diagnostics panel lists each error and warning with its line.\n\n## Complete example\n\n```xdbml\nxdbml: 0.6\n\nProject service_network {\n  targets: PostgreSQL\n  Note: 'Car service network: customers, vehicles, work orders and invoicing'\n}\n\nEnum work_order_status {\n  scheduled   [note: 'Appointment booked']\n  in_progress\n  on_hold     [note: 'Awaiting parts']\n  completed\n  invoiced\n}\n\nTable customers {\n  customer_id serial       [pk, not null]\n  full_name   varchar(120) [not null, synonyms: ['client name']]\n  email       varchar(255) [unique, not null, pattern: '^[^@]+@[^@]+$', tags: ['pii']]\n  created_at  timestamptz  [not null, default: `now()`]\n  Note: 'One row per registered customer'\n}\n\nTable vehicles {\n  vehicle_id  serial      [pk, not null]\n  customer_id int         [not null]\n  vin         varchar(17) [unique, not null, minLength: 17, maxLength: 17]\n  make        varchar(60)\n  model       varchar(60)\n  model_year  smallint    [check: 'model_year >= 1900']\n}\n\nTable service_centers {\n  center_id serial      [pk, not null]\n  name      varchar(80) [not null]\n  city      varchar(80)\n}\n\nTable work_orders {\n  order_id   serial            [pk, not null]\n  vehicle_id int               [not null]\n  center_id  int               [not null]\n  status     work_order_status [not null, default: 'scheduled']\n  opened_on  date              [not null]\n  closed_on  date\n  constraints {\n    'closed_on IS NULL OR closed_on >= opened_on' [name: 'chk_work_orders_dates']\n  }\n}\n\nTable work_order_lines {\n  order_id    int           [not null]\n  line_no     int           [not null]\n  description varchar(200)  [not null]\n  labour_hrs  decimal(5,2)  [minimum: 0]\n  parts_cost  decimal(12,2) [minimum: 0]\n  constraints {\n    (order_id, line_no) [pk, name: 'pk_work_order_lines']\n  }\n}\n\nTable invoices {\n  invoice_id serial        [pk, not null]\n  order_id   int           [unique, not null]\n  issued_on  date          [not null, granularity: day]\n  total      decimal(12,2) [not null, business_term: 'Invoice Total']\n}\n\nTable payments {\n  payment_id serial        [pk, not null]\n  invoice_id int           [not null]\n  paid_at    timestamptz   [not null]\n  amount     decimal(12,2) [not null, check: 'amount > 0']\n}\n\nRef fk_vehicles_customer: vehicles.customer_id > customers.customer_id [\n  source: '0..*',\n  target: '1..1',\n  source_role: 'owned vehicle',\n  target_role: 'owner'\n]\n\nRef fk_work_orders_vehicle: work_orders.vehicle_id > vehicles.vehicle_id [source: '0..*', target: '1..1']\n\nRef fk_work_orders_center: work_orders.center_id > service_centers.center_id [source: '0..*', target: '1..1']\n\nRef fk_work_order_lines_order: work_order_lines.order_id > work_orders.order_id [\n  source: '1..*',\n  target: '1..1',\n  delete: cascade,\n  constraint_type: identifying\n]\n\nRef fk_invoices_order: invoices.order_id - work_orders.order_id [source: '0..1', target: '1..1']\n\nRef fk_payments_invoice: payments.invoice_id > invoices.invoice_id [source: '0..*', target: '1..1']\n\nView center_monthly_revenue [materialized: true, refresh_schedule: 'monthly'] {\n  source_query: '''\n    SELECT wo.center_id, date_trunc('month', i.issued_on) AS month, SUM(i.total) AS revenue\n    FROM invoices i\n    JOIN work_orders wo ON wo.order_id = i.order_id\n    GROUP BY wo.center_id, date_trunc('month', i.issued_on)\n  '''\n  center_id int           [not null]\n  month     date          [not null, granularity: month]\n  revenue   decimal(14,2) [tags: ['kpi']]\n  Note: 'One row per service center and month'\n}\n```\n\n## More\n\n- Full specification, current draft: https://xdbml.org/spec/current\n- 5-minute introduction: https://xdbml.org/learn/\n- Live playground: https://xdbml.org/playground/\n- Repository: https://github.com/xdbml/xdbml-spec\n";
